> Well, at least to abstract datatype designers and many programmers including me, things like removing an element from an empty stack is an error (API abuse),

I also disagree. A common idiom for stacks or queues is to iterate over them until they are empty:
```
while ((item = OPENSSL_sk_pop(stack)) != NULL) {
    /* do work */
}
```
It is also more performant than checking `OPENSSL_sk_num()` first.

@t8m wrote:

> It was my serious mistake that I did not recognize the problem when approving the commit that caused the regression.

In contrast, I agree with @paulidale that the regression was actually caused by an oversight by me (and by the reviewers) : I missed a sloppy/wrong use of the stack API somewhere in the PKCS#12 parser, which could have been fixed in the same way as the other occurrences that I did handle.

@paulidale No, these are public interfaces! We cannot blindly add error raises to these.

They can be added if something is a genuine error condition, i.e. there is no reason to ever have such call in error-free code. However all these additions removed by this PR can happen in error-free code when you just depend on some of the existing behavior of these functions. If, and only if, these call conditions were always documented to be basically an undefined behavior, or if we always documented that error can be raised in these conditions, then it would be OK to add it.

@DDvO how would you fix similar "problems" with stack usage in 3rd party code???

@slontis, IMO we need an exception to remove the new errors.  I don't see this as taking a long time: we'll do it on the next OTC call as a matter of priority.

We will then have time to update policies etc at our leisure.

As to which option, I'm not too fussed to be honest.  I've been pushing for the one that doesn't require a vote here because it's easier and we know we've serious deficiencies in regards to error raising.
